WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.865 --> 00:00:13.611
inventors often leave behind accurate, written accounts of their great discoveries, it is not unusual for myths to

2
00:00:13.711 --> 00:00:17.755
spring up around the circumstances surrounding these momentous events.

3
00:00:19.056 --> 00:00:26.262
In the case of the creation of the telephone, on March 10, 1876, Alexander Graham Bell and his

4
00:00:26.362 --> 00:00:27.743
partner, Thomas A.

5
00:00:27.803 --> 00:00:34.936
Watson, attempted to transmit a voice between two adjoining rooms in a house using electrical current.

6
00:00:35.857 --> 00:00:40.400
Bell thought that a receiver wire dipped in acid would strengthen the current.

7
00:00:41.361 --> 00:00:46.704
Legend has it that he accidentally spilled some acid on himself and called to his partner in the

8
00:00:46.764 --> 00:00:48.566
other room for help: "Mr.

9
00:00:48.606 --> 00:00:49.686
Watson, come here.

10
00:00:50.087 --> 00:00:50.987
I want to see you."

11
00:00:51.868 --> 00:00:55.811
Watson heard Bell's famous words through the wire and came running.

12
00:00:56.851 --> 00:01:00.069
As it turned out, Bell didn't burn himself with acid.

13
00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:06.492
He had heard sounds coming through the wire the minute Watson had finished setting things up at his

14
00:01:06.652 --> 00:01:12.874
end. Watson hadn't said a word, but Bell wanted to let him know the invention worked!

15
00:01:14.135 --> 00:01:21.298
Given his surname, it seems only natural that Alexander Graham Bell, born in Edinburgh, Scotland, on March 3,

16
00:01:21.398 --> 00:01:22.359
1847, would be interested in sound.

17
00:01:26.384 --> 00:01:34.468
Alexander's father and grandfather were celebrated professors of "elocution," which can be compared to public speaking today.

18
00:01:35.768 --> 00:01:43.711
Alexander's father published an important book called Visible Speech, about how to teach speech to the deaf with

19
00:01:43.771 --> 00:01:48.774
a system of cryptic symbols that showed vocal sounds and how to make them.

20
00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:56.849
Bell's mother, Eliza, who started to lose her hearing at about age 12, also had a profound influence

21
00:01:56.909 --> 00:01:57.890
on her son's life.

22
00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:03.833
She proved to him that intelligence had no relationship to hearing ability.

23
00:02:04.874 --> 00:02:11.838
Born with a rich, expressive voice, Bell found a way to communicate with her by speaking in low,

24
00:02:12.318 --> 00:02:15.280
resonant tones aimed toward her forehead.

25
00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:23.512
He believed the vibrations in his voice were more important than loudness to communicate to her effectively, an

26
00:02:23.692 --> 00:02:26.854
insight that proved invaluable in his later work.

27
00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:36.278
Obsessed with sound, Alexander and his brother Melville studied the human airway and the tongue, and as teenagers,

28
00:02:36.618 --> 00:02:43.041
created a crude speaking machine out of rubber, wood, and wire that could say "Ma ma!"

29
00:02:44.141 --> 00:02:49.944
The boys were delighted when a houseguest heard the sound and started looking for a baby calling for

30
00:02:50.004 --> 00:02:50.504
its mother.

31
00:02:51.564 --> 00:02:58.806
Bell's curiosity grew as he got older, and he experimented with tuning forks, trying to measure the pitches

32
00:02:58.966 --> 00:02:59.966
of vocal sounds.

33
00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:08.749
At the age of 18, he was nominated for membership in the London Philological Society, a scholarly group

34
00:03:09.109 --> 00:03:14.370
devoted to the study of speech and language, a field known today as linguistics.

35
00:03:15.471 --> 00:03:23.109
He met a scientist who recommended a book by Hermann von Helmholtz, a German mathematician whose research involved

36
00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:23.989
tuning forks.

37
00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:29.511
The book, On Sensations of Tone, changed Bell's life.

38
00:03:30.731 --> 00:03:37.293
He noted that Helmholtz had created an electric tuning-fork device that could produce a vowel sound.

39
00:03:38.273 --> 00:03:46.556
Bell reasoned that if electricity could produce a vowel sound, it could generate consonants, music—any sound at all.

40
00:03:47.628 --> 00:03:49.890
Bell grew up in a dangerous world.

41
00:03:51.031 --> 00:03:58.897
Diseases like scarlet fever, the measles, and the mumps frequently caused deafness, and the smoggy air of Edinburgh

42
00:03:58.977 --> 00:04:01.299
created health problems for the Bell family.

43
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:10.406
After both Bell's brothers died from tuberculosis, the family decided to migrate to Brantford, Ontario, in Canada, in

44
00:04:10.506 --> 00:04:11.787
search of a better climate.

45
00:04:12.908 --> 00:04:20.935
There, a converted carriage house became his workshop; behind it, in a pleasant spot overlooking a river, Bell

46
00:04:21.015 --> 00:04:24.839
set aside a "dreaming place" where he could think about his ideas.

47
00:04:25.879 --> 00:04:29.463
Years later, he would use a houseboat as his "thinking place."

48
00:04:30.664 --> 00:04:37.990
In April 1871, Bell became a speech teacher at a noted Boston school for the deaf, and his

49
00:04:38.091 --> 00:04:44.812
success as a teacher made him famous in the deaf community, leading Helen Keller's father to ask him

50
00:04:44.892 --> 00:04:47.834
for help in finding the right teacher for his daughter.

51
00:04:48.695 --> 00:04:51.716
Bell helped the Keller family find Anne Sullivan.

52
00:04:52.717 --> 00:04:59.780
When Helen Keller visited Bell years later in 1901, she gushed, "He makes you feel if you only

53
00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.022
had a little more time, you too might be an inventor."

54
00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:12.767
In October 1874, Bell began a partnership with Thomas Sanders and Gardiner Greene Hubbard, the fathers of two

55
00:05:12.827 --> 00:05:13.548
of his students.

56
00:05:14.784 --> 00:05:22.990
The men financed his experiments, hoping to improve upon the all-important telegraph, the long-distance communication device that used

57
00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.292
coded impulses transmitted through electrical wires.

58
00:05:27.513 --> 00:05:31.715
Back then, a telegraph wire could send only one message at a time.

59
00:05:32.716 --> 00:05:37.940
Bell believed he could find a way to transmit multiple messages at the same time.

60
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:46.309
Many other inventors were working on this same idea, called a "harmonic telegraph," and the competition was fierce.

61
00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:55.094
Although Bell was not awarded the patent for the harmonic telegraph, his experiments eventually led to the creation

62
00:05:55.314 --> 00:05:56.154
of the telephone.

63
00:05:57.235 --> 00:06:02.178
He was so excited about his work on the telephone that he wrote to his father on February

64
00:06:02.358 --> 00:06:07.561
29, 1876, "If I succeed in securing that patent.

65
00:06:08.141 --> 00:06:14.889
the whole thing is mine and I am sure of fame, fortune, and success if I can only

66
00:06:14.969 --> 00:06:17.350
persevere in perfecting my apparatus."

67
00:06:18.831 --> 00:06:25.115
Meanwhile, Bell fell in love with Mabel Hubbard, one of his deaf students and Hubbard's daughter.

68
00:06:26.296 --> 00:06:35.522
Hubbard, a wealthy lawyer and former congressman, made certain Bell's discoveries were sufficiently documented, and his patent applications

69
00:06:35.642 --> 00:06:38.504
properly written and filed as early as possible.

70
00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:45.282
Concerned that Bell would lose the rights to all the results of his hard work, Hubbard arranged to

71
00:06:45.342 --> 00:06:52.263
apply for the telephone patent without Bell's consent, and the patent was actually issued before Bell had his

72
00:06:52.283 --> 00:06:54.084
"eureka moment" with Watson.

73
00:06:55.144 --> 00:07:01.286
Afterward, Bell married Mabel Hubbard and gave most of his shares in the new Bell Telephone Company to

74
00:07:01.346 --> 00:07:06.727
her. Bell's patent may be the most valuable single patent ever issued.

75
00:07:07.628 --> 00:07:13.491
Over the next few decades, it was legally challenged over six hundred times, but it prevailed.

76
00:07:14.631 --> 00:07:20.415
Bell later wrote to his wife, "Why should it matter to the world who invented the telephone so

77
00:07:20.475 --> 00:07:22.516
long as the world gets the benefit of it?

78
00:07:23.657 --> 00:07:28.299
Why should it matter to me what the world says on the subject so long as I have

79
00:07:28.439 --> 00:07:33.663
obtained the object for which I labored and have got you, my sweet darling wife?"

80
00:07:34.843 --> 00:07:39.117
Throughout his life, Bell had a love of learning that he was eager to share.

81
00:07:40.178 --> 00:07:47.241
The money his success brought him helped him to create Science magazine and the National Geographic Society.

82
00:07:48.282 --> 00:07:52.584
His advice to young people was not to walk through life with their eyes shut.

83
00:07:52.604 --> 00:07:59.287
"There are things around us and right at our very feet that we have never seen, because we

84
00:07:59.307 --> 00:08:00.508
have never really looked."

85
00:08:01.628 --> 00:08:07.290
It is no exaggeration to say that Bell's invention of the telephone changed the world, but Bell was

86
00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:10.190
also a pioneer in the field of fiber optics.

87
00:08:11.291 --> 00:08:18.473
Other inventions by Bell included a vacuum jacket, an iron-lung-type breathing apparatus that he worked on after his

88
00:08:18.513 --> 00:08:26.495
sons died in infancy of respiratory problems; and a forerunner of contemporary metal detectors that he invented in

89
00:08:26.575 --> 00:08:28.915
hopes of locating the bullet that killed U.S.

90
00:08:28.975 --> 00:08:30.516
President James Garfield.

91
00:08:31.788 --> 00:08:39.270
Bell studied condensation, worked on finding ways to remove the salt from seawater, and predicted that air pollution

92
00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:42.431
from fossil fuels might produce a "greenhouse effect."

93
00:08:43.391 --> 00:08:48.713
He loved to spend his summers working in his laboratory on Nova Scotia's Cape Breton Island.

94
00:08:48.733 --> 00:08:53.194
"There seems to be always something going where I am," he said.

95
00:08:53.214 --> 00:09:00.096
"Nothing, perhaps, that would interest other people, but it keeps me busy and interested all the time."

96
00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:09.011
When Bell died on August 2, 1922, telephones all throughout the United States and Canada fell silent for

97
00:09:09.191 --> 00:09:10.413
one minute in tribute.
